           deploy_stamp: desktop_lxc
  Play 28: desktop_configure (desktop_nodes) tag: desktop
  Play 29: kiosk_lxc (desktop_nodes)       tag: kiosk
           deploy_stamp: kiosk_lxc
  Play 30: kiosk_configure (desktop_nodes) tag: kiosk
  Play 30a: Distribute Cluster Manager SSH key (kiosk_nodes) tag: kiosk
  Play 31: gaming_lxc (gaming_nodes)       tag: gaming
           deploy_stamp: gaming_lxc
  Play 32: gaming_lxc_configure (gaming_nodes) tag: gaming

Phase 3c: Post-configure hookscript attachment
  Play 33: desktop_nodes — Attach display-exclusive hookscript to
           DRI-sharing containers/VMs (Kodi, Kiosk, Moonlight, Desktop)
           tag: kiosk, desktop, media, moonlight

Phase 3d: Heartbeat circuit breaker (post-configure)
  Play 34: localhost — Query /api/fleet/stale, hard-fail if any
           previously-healthy container stopped heartbeating

Per-feature plays (run in default flow; also invocable via --tags)
Bootstrap cleanup (proxmox:!lan_hosts)    tag: cleanup
```
When adding a new VM type, add a new provision play targeting the appropriate
flavor group, add a configure play targeting the dynamic group, and include
`deploy_stamp` in the provision play. Service plays go in Phase 3 so they
run on both primary and LAN hosts.

Post-baseline features are added as separate task files in the configure role
(e.g., `roles/openwrt_configure/tasks/security.yml`). Each feature gets a
PAIR of plays in `site.yml`: (1) configure on the dynamic group, (2) `deploy_stamp`
on the flavor group. Both share a tag so they can be invoked individually via
`--tags`, and they also run in the default (no-tags) flow alongside every other
service. NEVER use Ansible's `never` special tag — it silently skips plays in
the default E2E pipeline, creating untested dead code.

Dynamic groups populated by `add_host` are ephemeral. Any entry point that
runs as a separate `ansible-playbook` invocation (per-feature molecule
converge/verify, cleanup/rollback) MUST reconstruct the group first.
Extract reconstruction into `tasks/reconstruct_<type>_group.yml`.

## Network topology

A **cluster** is a single household's network. The router node creates a
LAN subnet (10.10.10.x). All nodes converge onto this subnet once the
WiFi mesh is established.

### Bootstrap (pre-mesh, Ansible provisioning)
```
ISP Router (192.168.86.x supernet)
  |
Switch
  |            |                  |            |            |
Home          AI Node          Mesh2       Bridge-1     Bridge-2
(primary)     192.168.86.220   192.168.86.211  .230        .231
  |
  |-- OpenWrt VM (10.10.10.1)
  |     |
  |     LAN bridge (10.10.10.x)
  |       |
  |     Mesh1 (10.10.10.210)
```

- **home**, **ai**, **mesh2**, **bridge-1**, **bridge-2**: directly reachable on the supernet (no proxy)
- **mesh1**: behind home's OpenWrt, reachable via ProxyCommand through home
- WAN hosts use NAT bridges (10.99.x.x) for container networking before mesh

### Operational (post-mesh, all nodes on LAN)
```
Cluster "Home Lab" — all nodes on 10.10.10.x

  OpenWrt VM (10.10.10.1) — DHCP, firewall, WDS AP
  |
  ├── home (10.10.10.2)     — wired, Cluster Manager
  ├── mesh1 (10.10.10.210)  — wired via switch
  ├── ai (10.10.10.x)       — WiFi mesh STA
  ├── mesh2 (10.10.10.x)    — WiFi mesh STA + wired backhaul via bridge-2
  ├── bridge-1 (10.10.10.x) — WiFi bridge AP (eth0→vmbr0, WiFi→bridge-2)
  └── bridge-2 (10.10.10.x) — WiFi bridge STA (WiFi→bridge-1, eth0→vmbr1→cable→mesh2)

  Bridge backhaul path:
    household LAN → bridge-1 vmbr0 → CT104 br-lan → WiFi AP
        ~~~~ dedicated 2.4GHz ch11 link ~~~~
    bridge-2 WiFi STA → CT104 br-lan → vmbr1 → cable → mesh2 vmbr1
```

CRITICAL: bridge-1 CT on vmbr0 (WAN bridge), bridge-2 CT on vmbr1
(backhaul bridge). NEVER both on vmbr0 — creates L2 broadcast storm.

All inter-Manager communication uses these LAN IPs directly.

## Device flavors (inventory groups)
Hosts belong to child groups under `proxmox` that determine which services they receive.
Shared infrastructure (backup, bridges, PCI, iGPU) targets `proxmox` and runs on every host.
Service-specific provision plays target flavor groups.
- `router_nodes` — OpenWrt router VM (home only)
- `vpn_nodes` — WireGuard VPN (home, mesh1, ai, mesh2 — all 4 nodes)
- `dns_nodes` — Pi-hole
- `wifi_nodes` — WDS WiFi backhaul (router creates hidden WDS AP; OpenWrt Mesh LXC on wifi_nodes:!router_nodes runs WDS STA)
- `monitoring_nodes` — Netdata, rsyslog
- `service_nodes` — Home Assistant
- `media_nodes` — Jellyfin, Kodi (home only — media server and player)
- `streaming_nodes` — Moonlight streaming client (mesh1 — MUST be on a different host than Sunshine server)
- `desktop_nodes` — Desktop VM (home only — display output host)
- `kiosk_nodes` — Manager/Kiosk LXC (ALL hosts — MUST equal `proxmox` membership. Every host needs a Manager for 4-tier heartbeat)
- `gaming_nodes` — Gaming LXC with GPU render device sharing (ai only; uses Fedora + Sunshine + dsda-doom)
- `bridge_nodes` — Dedicated WiFi Bridge LXC (bridge-1 AP, bridge-2 STA — transparent L2 WDS link with wired backhaul to mesh2). AP container on WAN bridge (vmbr0), STA container on backhaul bridge (vmbr1). NEVER both on vmbr0 — causes L2 broadcast storm.
- `lan_hosts` — Satellite Proxmox nodes behind the OpenWrt router (accessed via ProxyCommand; mesh1 only)
A host can belong to multiple flavor groups.

## Deployment tracking
The `deploy_stamp` role writes `/etc/ansible/facts.d/vm_builds.fact` on each
Proxmox host after each play. On subsequent runs, `ansible_local.vm_builds`
contains the project version, last run timestamp, and per-play history.
Bump `project_version` in `group_vars/all.yml` when cutting a release.

## VMID allocation
- 100-199: Network (100 OpenWrt, 101 WireGuard, 102 Pi-hole, 103 Mesh WiFi, 104 WiFi Bridge)
- 200-299: Services (200 Home Assistant)
- 300-399: Media (300 Jellyfin, 301 Kodi, 302 Moonlight)
- 400-499: Desktop (400 Desktop VM, 401 Kiosk)
- 500-599: Observability (500 Netdata, 501 rsyslog)
- 600-699: Gaming (600 Gaming VM legacy, 601 Gaming LXC active)
- 999: reserved for molecule test containers
All VMIDs defined in `group_vars/all.yml`.

## Variable scoping
- **Required secrets**: `.env` file → `lookup('env', 'VAR_NAME')` in `group_vars/proxmox.yml`
- **API tokens**: `<HOSTNAME>_API_TOKEN` in `.env`/`test.env` (e.g., `HOME_API_TOKEN`, `MESH1_API_TOKEN`, `AI_API_TOKEN`, `MESH2_API_TOKEN`). `group_vars/proxmox.yml` resolves dynamically via `(inventory_hostname | upper | replace('-', '_')) + '_API_TOKEN'`.
- **Primary host**: `PRIMARY_HOST` in `.env`/`test.env` — the entry point IP that `build.py` probes.
- **Additional host IPs**: `AI_HOST`, `MESH_2_HOST` in `.env`/`test.env` for directly reachable nodes.
- **Optional env vars**: `.env` file → `lookup('env', 'VAR_NAME') | default('', true)` in role `defaults/main.yml` (e.g., `WAN_MAC`)
- **Web UI / API port**: `WEBUI_PORT` in `.env`/`test.env` (default: 52500). Controls the NiceGUI web UI, callhome API, and headless API server ports. MUST be in a range open in the controller's firewall (currently 52500-53000). `build.py`, `app.py`, and `start_test_api.py` all read this env var.
- **Generated secrets and dynamic config**: Auto-generated during runs → written to `{{ env_generated_path }}` on the controller via `blockinfile`. Resolves to `test.env.generated` under Molecule, `.env.generated` in production. Includes auto-generated keys (WireGuard) and dynamic runtime config (LAN_GATEWAY, LAN_CIDR). See `secret-generation` rule.
- **Callhome server URL**: Written to `.state/callhome_url` by `build.py` (production), `prepare.yml` (test), or the NiceGUI app on startup. Read by `callhome_server` in `group_vars/all.yml` via `lookup('file')`. Hard-fails if missing. NEVER set via env var or hardcode in `.env`/`test.env`.
- **Shared VM params**: `group_vars/all.yml` (VMID, image path, storage, `project_root`, `env_generated_path`, `callhome_server`)
- **Proxmox connection**: `group_vars/proxmox.yml` (API auth, SSH settings)
- **Per-host overrides**: `host_vars/<hostname>.yml` (IP, reboot policy)
- **LAN host SSH**: `group_vars/lan_hosts.yml` (ProxyCommand through primary host with keepalives on both hops)
- **Role defaults**: `roles/<role>/defaults/main.yml` (never cross-reference between roles)
- **Cross-role facts**: `set_fact` with `cacheable: true` or `add_host` variables

