## Image and template management
- Per the Design principles above: every VM and container uses a purpose-built image with all packages baked in. Images are REQUIRED — provision roles verify existence and hard-fail if missing. Configure roles NEVER install packages at runtime.
- ALL images are stored locally in `images/` (gitignored) and uploaded to Proxmox during provisioning. NEVER depend on remote downloads from the Proxmox host.
- `build-images.sh` is the current image build script (OpenWrt via Image Builder). Future services will have their own build steps (Dockerfiles, Packer, debootstrap, etc.).
- VM images: uploaded to a temp directory, decompressed if `.gz`, imported via `qm importdisk`, then deleted. Use `/var/tmp/` for images >5 GB (Windows qcow2 are 8-18 GB); `/tmp/` (tmpfs) is too small.
- LXC templates: uploaded to `/var/lib/vz/template/cache/` via `ansible.builtin.copy`. The `proxmox_lxc` role checks `pveam list` and skips upload when the template is already cached.
- NEVER delete LXC templates in molecule cleanup. Templates are large (140-315MB each) and identical between runs. Deleting them forces re-upload of ~820MB across all hosts on every test cycle. Template deletion is ONLY valid in production cleanup behind `[full-restore, clean]` tags.
- NEVER use `pveam download` — the host may lack internet (router VM destroyed) or have unreachable enterprise repos (no subscription).
- Image paths are defined in `group_vars/all.yml` (e.g., `openwrt_image_path`, `openwrt_lxc_template_path`). Roles verify the image exists and fail with a clear message pointing to `./build-images.sh` if missing.
- ALWAYS use `role_path` for source paths in tasks. Bare relative paths break when Molecule runs from non-default scenarios.
- Use `./build-images.sh --host <ip> --only <target>` to rebuild a single image on a specific host (~2-3 min vs ~15 min for all). Targets: mesh, router, pihole, rsyslog, netdata, wireguard, homeassistant, kodi, moonlight, gaming, sunshine, desktop.
- Build images IN PARALLEL across the 6 test hosts. Each host can build one image at a time. Different images on different hosts simultaneously.
- NEVER skip the image rebuild step after changing `build-images.sh`. Running `molecule test` with stale images will fail because configure roles expect baked content.
- NEVER add "legacy image fallback" code in configure roles. If the image lacks baked content, REBUILD THE IMAGE.
- See `vm-lifecycle` skill, Image management section, for the full pattern.

## NTP synchronization
- ALWAYS check clock skew BEFORE running the full NTP sync sequence. Use `chronyc -n tracking | awk '/System time/{print $4}'` to measure skew. Only sync when skew > 30 seconds.
- NEVER run unconditional NTP burst+sleep+makestep sequences. Each sync takes ~7s per host. With 4 hosts and multiple sync points, unconditional syncs waste 60-90s when clocks are accurate.
- Set `cache_valid_time: 86400` (24h) for `ansible.builtin.apt` tasks on test machines, not 3600 (1h). Short intervals trigger unnecessary `apt-get update` calls between test cycles.

## pct_remote performance
- Each `pct_remote` task opens a new paramiko SSH connection to the Proxmox host, then spawns `pct exec`. Overhead: 15-60s per task.
- MINIMIZE configure role task count for LXC containers. Base system config that is identical across containers MUST be baked into the image, not deployed via the configure role.
- Configure roles should ONLY contain host-specific config (IPs, streaming endpoints, passwords, optional features gated on env vars).
- Previous bug: systemd override deployed via configure role (3 tasks × 4 nodes = 12 pct_remote calls) added ~12 minutes. Moving to image-only saved 38% of per-feature test time.
- ALWAYS batch file pushes into a single tarball when deploying multiple files into containers. Create tar on the host → single `pct push` → single `pct exec` to extract. Never loop individual `pct push` calls.
- Previous improvement: kiosk_configure originally ran 14 individual `pct push` calls per host. Batching into a single tarball reduced pct operations from 17 to 2, improving reliability on USB ethernet hosts and reducing configure time.

## Hardware detection: hard-fail over graceful degradation
- ALWAYS prefer hard-fail when expected hardware is missing. Graceful degradation masks real problems.
- iGPU: REQUIRED on every host. `proxmox_igpu` hard-fails if absent. Supports both Intel (i915) and AMD (amdgpu).
- WiFi + VT-d/IOMMU: REQUIRED for PCI passthrough. `proxmox_pci_passthrough` hard-fails if IOMMU is not active after reboot or if IOMMU groups are invalid.
- NIC count: OK to handle dynamically — hardware legitimately varies (1 NIC on mesh satellites, 3+ on router nodes). The 2-bridge minimum is gated on `router_nodes` group membership.
- NIC link state: NEVER create bridges for NICs with no carrier (no cable connected). `proxmox_bridges` checks `carrier` sysfs before bridge creation. Disconnected NICs leave broken config that fails on reboot.
- Previous bug: `proxmox_pci_passthrough` "gracefully skipped" WiFi passthrough when IOMMU groups were invalid. The real cause was VT-d disabled in BIOS on mesh1. The silent skip masked a fixable BIOS setting for an entire test cycle.
- Previous bug (2026-04-08): `proxmox_bridges` created a bridge for ai's PCIe NIC (r8169) that had no cable connected. On reboot, the bridge config referenced a NIC that didn't initialize, risking PVE service failures. Note: the SSH instability on ai during this session was separately caused by a kiosk container IP collision (.220) — see WAN container IP collision rule.

## Baseline workflow
- The OpenWrt baseline on the primary host is expensive to rebuild (~4 min). Layered scenarios (`mesh1-infra`, `openwrt-security`, etc.) depend on it.
- Use `molecule converge` + `molecule verify` for day-to-day iteration. This leaves the baseline running so leaf nodes (mesh1) remain accessible.
- Use `molecule test` for clean-state validation (CI, pre-commit). The test_sequence runs: `dependency → cleanup → syntax → prepare → converge → verify`. There is NO trailing converge or cleanup — the baseline is left running after verify.
- NEVER add `cleanup` or `destroy` to the end of the test_sequence. That tears down OpenWrt and makes mesh1 permanently unreachable.

## Testing requirements
- ALWAYS run `molecule test` after changing roles, playbooks, or molecule config
- ALWAYS update `molecule/default/verify.yml` with assertions for new/changed behavior
- Verify completeness: every configured feature needs an assertion ("VM is running" is NOT sufficient — also check DHCP, NIC topology, auto-start, services, backup manifest, state files)
- NEVER consider a task complete until `molecule test` passes with exit code 0
- ALWAYS run `pytest tests/ -v` after modifying `build.py` — every public function and error path needs coverage
- Run `ansible-lint && yamllint .` before deploying to hardware
- See `.cursor/skills/ansible-testing/SKILL.md` for full details

### INVARIANT: Manual testing requires a fully converged system
Manual testing (browser UI, CLI playbooks, API queries) MUST ONLY run
after `molecule test` has completed successfully and ALL 6 hosts are on
the 10.10.10.x LAN with ALL containers deployed and heartbeating.

NEVER start manual testing in a pre-mesh or partially deployed state.
NEVER rationalize "No route to host" errors as "expected." If the system
is not converged, run `molecule test` first — no exceptions.

The system ALWAYS ends `molecule test` in a pristine, fully deployed
state. Manual testing starts from that pristine state. Build first,
test second. Always.

Previous catastrophe (2026-04-10): Agent started manual testing before
running molecule test. Batman enable, WiFi status, fleet queries all
returned "No route to host" for WAN hosts. Wasted the entire session.

## SHOW STOPPER: Unreachable host protocol
- When ANY host shows `unreachable=1`, fails a connectivity probe, OR SSH auth is rejected: FULL STOP. Do not continue. Do not build other hosts. Do not "skip and continue."
- NEVER dismiss as "pre-existing." NEVER say "not caused by our changes." Investigate immediately.
- For `wol_capable: false` hosts (ai): physical power-on required 3000 miles away. No remote recovery.
- Do NOT validate features against a substitute host. If ai runs Sunshine/Doom and ai is down, Moonlight verification is IMPOSSIBLE.
- ALWAYS run SSH checks to ALL hosts after every build/deploy, not just the target.
- SSH "Permission denied" is a SHOW STOPPER — treat identically to EHOSTUNREACH.
- Previous catastrophes: (1) agent dismissed ai unreachable THREE TIMES over 4 hours, wasting the entire session. (2) agent continued building 4 other hosts while ai SSH was failing, instead of stopping immediately.

## Cleanup philosophy: unified, service-specific, not scorched-earth
- `playbooks/cleanup.yml` is the SINGLE cleanup playbook for all contexts (molecule, CLI, SuperManager). `molecule/default/cleanup.yml` is a one-line import of it.
- Cleanup MUST destroy VMs/containers using **explicit VMIDs** from `group_vars/all.yml`. NEVER iterate `qm list` / `pct list` to destroy ALL resources. Check existence first with `qm status` / `pct status`, then stop + destroy.
- When adding a new service, add its VMID to the cleanup lists in `playbooks/cleanup.yml` (`project_ct_ids`/`project_vm_ids`) and `tasks/cleanup_lan_host.yml`. There is only ONE place now.
- Cleanup MUST NOT restore the host config from backup archive by default. Explicit file removal is sufficient. Backup restore is opt-in via `--tags restore -e rollback=true`.
- Rollback plays in cleanup.yml are gated by `when: rollback | default(false) | bool`. They NEVER run during default cleanup. Invoke via: `ansible-playbook cleanup.yml --tags <feature>-rollback -e rollback=true`.
- NEVER use Ansible's `never` special tag on ANY play. It silently skips plays in the default E2E pipeline, creating untested dead code. Every service play runs every time. Idempotency is the gate, not tag exclusion. Previous bug: Gaming LXC was tagged `[gaming, never]` from its first commit and was NEVER tested in any E2E run for over a month. All per-feature OpenWrt plays were also tagged `[never]` — none ran in the default pipeline.
- Previous bug: blanket `qm list` / `pct list` cleanup destroyed non-project resources, forced full rebuild of every VM/container on every test run, and was slower than explicit VMID lookup.
- When a provisioning role deploys host-level systemd units (socat proxies, relay services), add the unit name to BOTH `playbooks/cleanup.yml` (primary hosts) AND `tasks/cleanup_lan_host.yml` (LAN hosts). The main cleanup targets `proxmox:!lan_hosts` — LAN hosts are EXCLUDED and only get cleanup via `tasks/cleanup_lan_host.yml`.
- When RENAMING a systemd service, the provisioning role MUST stop and remove the old service BEFORE deploying the replacement. Without this, the old service holds the port and the new service crash-loops. Add a pre-deployment task: `systemctl stop/disable <old> && rm -f /etc/systemd/system/<old>.service && pkill -f 'socat.*TCP-LISTEN:<port>'`.
- Previous catastrophe (2026-04-14): KasmVNC migration renamed `kiosk-vnc-proxy` to `kiosk-display-proxy`. mesh1 (LAN host, excluded from main cleanup) kept the old service running on port 6080. New service crash-looped 957 times. Instead of diagnosing the port conflict (10 seconds to find via `systemctl status`), retries were added and increased to 100 seconds. The SM/CM heartbeat checks used 6 minutes EACH of retries. Total wasted time: 12+ minutes per molecule test run, across multiple runs, all because the root cause was never investigated.

## Systemd sandboxing in LXC containers
- Many Debian packages ship systemd unit files with sandboxing directives (`LogNamespace`, `ProtectSystem`, `ProtectHome`, `ProtectControlGroups`, `BindReadOnlyPaths`, `RuntimeDirectory`) that require mount namespace creation. Inside LXC containers, these fail with exit code 226 (NAMESPACE) unless the container has `nesting=1` feature.
- ALWAYS add `features: nesting=1` to LXC containers that run services with systemd sandboxing. This is NOT optional for Netdata, rsyslog, and most Debian services.
- Even with `nesting=1`, some directives like `LogNamespace` still fail. Bake a systemd drop-in override into the image during `build-images.sh` to disable problematic directives: `LogNamespace=`, `ProtectSystem=false`, `ProtectHome=false`, `ProtectControlGroups=false`, `BindReadOnlyPaths=`. Do NOT deploy this in the configure role — it's base system config, not host-specific topology.
- NEVER try to clear systemd directives with empty strings for boolean-like settings. Use explicit `false` values (e.g., `ProtectSystem=false`, not `ProtectSystem=`).
- Previous bug: Netdata service exited 226/NAMESPACE in an LXC container even as privileged. Root cause: `LogNamespace=netdata` in the Netdata systemd unit. Fix: nesting=1 feature + systemd override baked into image.
- Previous bug: systemd override deployed via configure role (pct_remote) added 3 slow tasks per container. With 4 nodes, this added ~12 minutes to molecule test. Moved to image-only.

## Host metrics via LXC bind mounts
- Bind mounting host `/proc` and `/sys` into LXC containers for monitoring requires BOTH: (1) privileged container (`unprivileged: false`) and (2) `nesting=1` feature.
- Mount entries use full Proxmox mount specs: `/proc,mp=/host/proc,ro=1` — the `ro=1` flag makes them read-only.
- The monitoring agent's config must point to `/host/proc` and `/host/sys` (baked into the image) so it reads host metrics, not container-local metrics.
- Previous bug: unprivileged container with bind mounts — extraction failed (disk full at 1GB) and systemd sandboxing prevented service startup.

## API-driven LXC provisioning requires template upload
- When LXC roles use the NM API (`/api/provision/<service>`) instead of `include_role: proxmox_lxc`, the Ansible role MUST upload the template to the Proxmox host's `/var/lib/vz/template/cache/` BEFORE calling the API. The NM API passes the template path to PVE API's `pct create`, which requires the template to already exist on the host.
- Use `tasks/upload_lxc_template.yml` to handle the upload. It checks if the template is cached and only uploads when missing.
- Previous bug (2026-04-21): WireGuard containers were destroyed during version-mismatch recreation, then `pct create` failed with "volume does not exist" because the template wasn't on the host. The old `proxmox_lxc` include_role handled uploads, but the API-driven migration lost this step.
- ALWAYS add a `pve_api.py` entry to the kiosk image's `tar_files` array in `build-images.sh` when adding new Python modules that `manager.py` imports.
- Previous bug (2026-04-21): `manager.py` imported `pve_api` but `pve_api.py` wasn't in the kiosk image's baked file list. All 6 kiosk-web services crash-looped with `ModuleNotFoundError`. Fix: added to `tar_files` array and rebuilt kiosk image.

## PVE SSL certificates and API health
- Proxmox standalone nodes can lose their SSL certificates (`/etc/pve/local/pve-ssl.key`) after hostname changes, OS reinstalls, or pmxcfs corruption. Without the key, pveproxy workers log "failed to load local private key" and all HTTPS API calls hang during SSL handshake (TCP connects but handshake times out).
- The infra pre_tasks in `site.yml` check for the SSL key and regenerate with `pvecm updatecerts --force` if missing, then restart pveproxy.
- Standalone nodes can also enter a state where `pvesh get /nodes/<hostname>/status` returns "proxy not allowed" (HTTP 500). This is caused by stale PVE service state — restarting `pve-cluster`, `pvedaemon`, and `pveproxy` fixes it.
- The infra pre_tasks also clean stale `/etc/pve/nodes/<old_name>/` directories left from hostname changes (e.g., mesh1 previously named "home").
- `PveApiError` uses `self.status` (not `self.status_code`) for the HTTP status code. ALWAYS use `exc.status` when handling `PveApiError` exceptions.
- Previous bug (2026-04-21): mesh2 and bridge-1 had missing SSL keys. All HTTPS API calls from their kiosk containers to the PVE API timed out during SSL handshake. Fix: `pvecm updatecerts --force` on both hosts.
- Previous bug (2026-04-21): mesh1's PVE API returned "proxy not allowed" for `/nodes/mesh1/lxc` even though the hostname was correct. Root cause: stale pvedaemon state. Fix: restart all PVE services.
- Previous bug (2026-04-21): `manager.py` used `exc.status_code` instead of `exc.status` when handling `PveApiError`. Fix: changed to `exc.status`.

## LXC container disk sizing
- ALWAYS verify the compressed template size fits the target rootfs. A vzdump archive reports its compressed and extracted sizes, but the rootfs must have headroom for runtime data.
- Netdata's Debian 12 template is ~314MB compressed, ~1013MB extracted. A 1GB rootfs is too small — extraction fails partway through (tar errors on SSL certs). Use 2GB minimum.
- Previous bug: Netdata template built via vzdump produced a 314MB archive. `pct create` with 1GB rootfs failed mid-extraction because the expanded content exceeded the disk.

## Wake-on-LAN recovery
- `wol.sh` is a utility script for remotely waking Proxmox hosts. It is a LAST RESORT for recovering from power outages — not a substitute for proper UPS or IPMI.
- WAN hosts (home, ai, mesh2) receive magic packets via local L2 broadcast from the controller.
- LAN hosts (mesh1) receive magic packets via proxy: the script SSHes to the primary host and sends the packet from there (Python 3 socket, no extra packages needed).
- WoL requires: (1) NIC with WoL support, (2) WoL enabled in BIOS, (3) `ethtool Wake-on: g` on the management NIC, (4) the machine in standby (S5) with power connected.
- USB ethernet adapters do NOT support WoL. The USB host controller is powered down in S5 and lacks magic packet detection circuitry. Hosts with USB-only networking (e.g., ai) require manual power-on or an alternative like a smart plug.
- NEVER test WoL as part of the regular test suite. WoL is destructive (requires the host to be down). Only test it manually or in a dedicated recovery scenario.

## Manager port forwarding for 4-tier heartbeat chain
- The kiosk Manager API (port 9001) must be reachable from child Managers on other hosts.
- **WAN hosts**: iptables DNAT `vmbr0:9001` → kiosk NAT IP (10.99.x.20:9001). Works because the kiosk and host share the same 10.99.x.x NAT bridge — no cross-bridge hairpin.
- **Router node (home)**: `socat TCP-LISTEN:9001,fork TCP:10.10.10.23:9001` — userspace TCP proxy. NEVER use iptables DNAT here because the kiosk is on vmbr1 (LAN) while child Managers connect via vmbr0 (WAN). DNAT between two bridges on the same host creates a hairpin NAT that Linux conntrack cannot handle: the reply from the kiosk goes to the host's local stack via L2 bridge delivery, bypassing conntrack's NAT reversal. The connection permanently stalls in SYN_RECV.
- **LAN hosts (mesh1)**: No forwarding needed — share the LAN with the ClusterManager.
- **MANAGEMENT_SERVER** for WAN kiosks uses the router node's `ansible_host` (WAN IP, e.g., 192.168.86.201:9001), NOT the kiosk's LAN IP. For LAN kiosks, uses the kiosk's LAN IP directly.
- NEVER use iptables DNAT+MASQUERADE between two Proxmox bridges (vmbr0→vmbr1) on the same host. The bridge delivers replies directly to the host's local IP via L2, conntrack sees the packet in INPUT with un-reversed NAT, and the connection hangs. Use socat for cross-bridge forwarding.
- Previous bug (2026-04-11): DNAT from vmbr0 to LAN kiosk (10.10.10.23) with MASQUERADE created hairpin NAT. Extensive debugging (LOG rules in raw/nat/filter chains, tcpdump on all interfaces, conntrack analysis) confirmed: SYN packets were DNAT'd and MASQ'd correctly. SYN-ACK replies arrived at the host via vmbr1 bridge but entered INPUT with original addresses — conntrack never reversed the NAT. The connection stuck in SYN_RECV for all 4 WAN child Managers. Fix: replaced iptables DNAT with socat TCP proxy on the router node.

## Kiosk container HOST_IP must be routable from the container
- Kiosk containers use `HOST_IP` to SSH back to their Proxmox host for `pct list`/`qm list`. This IP MUST be reachable from inside the container.
- **Router nodes** (home): use the computed LAN management IP (e.g., 10.10.10.2 from `_lan_gateway` + `proxmox_lan_mgmt_ip_offset`). The WAN IP is unreachable from the LAN bridge.
- **LAN hosts** (mesh1): use `ansible_host` directly — for `lan_hosts`, `ansible_host` IS the LAN IP (e.g., 10.10.10.210). NEVER apply the router node's fixed `.2` offset to non-router LAN hosts.
- **WAN containers**: use the NAT bridge gateway IP (10.99.{container_subnet_id}.1). The host IS the gateway for the container's NAT bridge.
- NEVER use `ansible_host` as `HOST_IP` for router_nodes — it's the WAN IP, unreachable from LAN containers. But for `lan_hosts`, `ansible_host` IS the correct LAN IP.
- Previous bug (2026-04-12): `HOST_IP` was set to `ansible_host` (192.168.86.201). The kiosk container on the LAN bridge (10.10.10.23) could not route to it. The Containers page showed blank because `_api_guests` SSH'd to an unreachable host. Fix: compute `_kiosk_host_ip` dynamically based on whether the host is a LAN or WAN node.
- Previous bug (2026-04-12): The HOST_IP formula used `_lan_gateway[0:3] + '.2'` for ALL hosts in `router_nodes` OR `lan_hosts`. mesh1's kiosk got HOST_IP=10.10.10.2 (home's IP) instead of 10.10.10.210 (mesh1's IP). All batman status, guest management, and container operations on mesh1 were silently SSHing to the wrong Proxmox host (home instead of mesh1). Fix: split the condition — router_nodes use the computed offset, lan_hosts use ansible_host.

## qm list vs pct list column order
- `pct list` columns: `VMID Status Lock Name` — status at index 1, name at index 2+
- `qm list` columns: `VMID NAME STATUS MEM BOOTDISK PID` — name at index 1, status at index 2
- `parse_guest_list()` detects the format from the header row (`NAME` as second column = qm format).
- Previous bug (2026-04-12): `parse_guest_list()` assumed `parts[1]=status, parts[2]=name` for both formats. VM 100 showed `name: "stopped"` and `status: "openwrt-router"` (swapped fields).

## Entry point convention
- `build.py` is the SINGLE entry point for running Ansible. ALL shell scripts MUST delegate to it.
- NEVER call `ansible-playbook` directly from `run.sh` or `cleanup.sh`.
- `build.py` handles: env file parsing (with quote stripping), required var validation, host probing with state file fallback, and playbook resolution. Bypassing it loses all of these.
- See `.cursor/skills/build-conventions/SKILL.md` for patterns and test requirements.
